Revision history for Ekorn-Pipeline

Unreleased

v0.5.0 2026-11-27

    Stages that hold values back say where what they give came from.

    So the stages after them get each value's own original (roadmap
    2026-0060). What take_last and batch give result_original
    changes, so it's a v0.5.0.

    result_original promises the original of the value a result is
    made from, but after a stage that holds values back, such as
    take_last or batch, the stages after it saw the original of the
    value the run had got to last. Stages written before this don't
    change.

    - A stage may say holds => 1 among its parts, by name. In its step,
      as it keeps a value, kept_origin returns the value's origin,
      whose gives marks what the stage made from it, so the stages
      after it get that value's original, in a flush too (roadmap
      2026-0060).
    - kept_origin is exported by Ekorn::Pipeline::Context, and by
      Tooling's :context.
    - take_last's value has its own original, where it had none.
    - A batch has no original, where one filled by a value had that
      value's.
    - Two new messages: "Expected stage #%s, made with gather, to hold
      values back in its step, not in a pipeline", and "Expected
      kept_origin in the step of a stage that holds values back".

v0.4.5 2026-11-26

    A fix to the tests: they pass without Text::CSV.

    Since v0.3.10, the tests failed wherever Text::CSV, which
    Ekorn::Pipeline::CSV only recommends, wasn't installed, as on many
    of CPAN Testers' smokers. It changes only the tests.

    - t/30-csv.t is skipped when Text::CSV isn't installed, as
      t/31-csv-without-text-csv.t already tests that case.

v0.4.4 2026-11-26

    A documentation release: the how-to's "Coming from DBIx::Squirrel"
    corrected.

    Two rows of the section described DBIx::Squirrel wrongly, as its
    session pointed out. It changes only the docs.

    - The how-to's "Coming from DBIx::Squirrel" no longer gives
      DBIx::Squirrel's iterators finish, which is Ekorn's alone, says
      its Tooling's tags hold their own lists, names is_stop as
      DBIx::Squirrel's alone, and adds $DumpSecrets, tied between the
      two.

v0.4.3 2026-11-26

    An iterator's next, and a way into the how-to for DBIx::Squirrel's
    users.

    Ekorn::Pipeline::Iterator has next, which does what calling the
    iterator does, so a loop written for DBIx::Squirrel's iterators
    reads Ekorn's too, and the how-to has a section for readers who
    come to it from DBIx::Squirrel. It ships roadmap 2026-0056 and
    2026-0057, and only adds.

    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Iterator's next returns the next value, as
      $next->() does, in any context: one value, or an empty list once
      the values run out, so while (my($value) = $next->next) { ... }
      reads every value. (roadmap 2026-0056)
    - The how-to's "Coming from DBIx::Squirrel" gives Ekorn's names
      for what DBIx::Squirrel's users know, says what's new to them
      and where to start, and the README points to it.
      (roadmap 2026-0057)

v0.4.2 2026-11-25

    An iterator's values as an array reference, whatever the context.

    Ekorn::Pipeline::Iterator has all_arrayref, which gives every value
    still to come as an array reference, in a hash, an argument list or
    a sub's return as well as in scalar context, where all would give a
    list. It ships roadmap 2026-0055, and only adds.

    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Iterator's all_arrayref returns every value
      still to come as an array reference, whatever the context it's
      called in, and an empty one if there are none: all's array
      reference, without its dependence on context, so
      { rows => $next->all_arrayref } can't be flattened. all is as it
      was. (roadmap 2026-0055)

v0.4.1 2026-11-25

    A documentation release: a new recipe in the how-to, and its advice
    on versions brought up to date.

    The how-to shows a module offering a dataset as a stream, a
    collection and an index, and advises requiring the v0.4 series,
    where v0.4.0's still advised v0.3's. It ships roadmap 2026-0054,
    and changes only the docs.

    - The how-to's advice on which versions of Ekorn::Pipeline to
      require gives >= v0.4.0, < v0.5.0, the current series, where it
      gave v0.3's.
    - The how-to's chapter 9 has a recipe, "Offer a stream, a collection
      and an index": a function giving a fresh run through the caller's
      stages, one giving every value read once and kept, and one giving
      an index a sink built once, and when each suits. (roadmap
      2026-0054)

v0.4.0 2026-11-25

    pipe gives what a pipeline gives, in either context.

    Ekorn::Pipeline::Pipes' pipe, in scalar context, gives how many
    results came out, or a sink's result, as a pipeline does, not the
    first result. It ships the rest of roadmap 2026-0053, and changes
    what pipe gives in scalar context, which code may depend on, so
    it's v0.4.0.

    - pipe gives what a pipeline of its stages, called with the object,
      gives: in scalar context, how many results came out, or a sink's
      result, a list sink's as an array reference. It gave the first
      result, or undef, unlike any other run: one of the first methods
      written, before a run's scalar context was settled. For one value,
      read it in list context, my($first) = $object->pipe(...), or end
      with keep_first. Test2's is takes its first argument in scalar
      context, so is $object->pipe(...), 42 now compares a count.
      DBIx::Squirrel's handles inherit pipe. (roadmap 2026-0053)

v0.3.16 2026-11-25

    A quality release, of what a review of the code, tests and docs found.

    A | chain passes on its source's original and context, as an
    iterator does, and reads into a sink faster; from_csv no longer
    warns at an undefined first field; every module has a $VERSION; and
    the reference's examples, and runs pushed, pulled and fed, are
    tested. Each change below is listed by its finding.

    It ships part of roadmap 2026-0053, whose record numbers the
    findings, F1 to F17; v0.4.0 brings the rest. It fixes and adds, and
    changes nothing a stage depends on.

    - F1: a run reading a chain made with |, given to iterator as its
      source, gets the chain's source's original, from result_original,
      and its context, as one reading an iterator of it does. It got
      the chain's own values as their originals. (roadmap 2026-0053)
    - F2: from_csv no longer warns, from inside Ekorn::Pipeline::CSV,
      where a first row's first field is undefined, as Text::CSV's
      blank_is_undef and empty_is_undef make an empty one: dropping a
      byte order mark from it read it. (roadmap 2026-0053)
    - F3: @{} and a chain into a sink read with the iterator's all,
      which takes a list sink's result at once, rather than a value at
      a time. 100,000 values, on Perl 5.36: @{} from 16.8 to 24.0 reads
      a second, and a chain of one stage into keep_all from 5.1 to
      10.1. (roadmap 2026-0053)
    - F4: a run with a flush no longer checks, at each value, whether
      a flush it hasn't reached yet has been called. (roadmap
      2026-0053)
    - F5: Ekorn::Pipeline makes a run's parts, and words a failed
      stage's or end's message, in one place each, where each was
      written out three times; and a run looks for each part's role
      method only as far as the first it offers. The messages are as
      they were. A short run, two stages and five values, is 2 to 3%
      quicker, pushed or pulled, on Perl 5.36; long runs cost what they
      did. (roadmap 2026-0053)
    - F6: every sub has one blank line above its comment, or two above
      it without one, in the modules, tests and scripts, as the style
      says; 110 had two above a comment. (roadmap 2026-0053)
    - F7: small slips of style: a section comment stranded between
      Sinks' subs, a blank line before each module's closing 1;, and
      Ekorn::Pipeline::Feed cleans its namespace, as the other classes
      do, so croakf isn't one of its methods. (roadmap 2026-0053)
    - F8: the notes that a name was "new in v0.3.0", 25 of them in the
      POD and the reference, are gone: v0.3.0 was only a trial, and
      they said the same thing over and over. Later notes stay.
      (roadmap 2026-0053)
    - F9: "Made for a run", in Ekorn::Pipeline's POD and the reference,
      lists a source's original, and the keys a set-up or role method
      can return its parts by include original, as they have since
      v0.3.13. (roadmap 2026-0053)
    - F10: Ekorn::Pipeline::Pipes links to Factories' pipeline, where
      it linked to a section of Ekorn::Pipeline's that doesn't exist.
      (roadmap 2026-0053)
    - F11: Ekorn::Pipeline's SEE ALSO names CSV, Context and Errors.
      (roadmap 2026-0053)
    - F12: the README no longer says, twice, that nothing is held
      between stages, as batch, collect and the like hold values; nor
      that Tooling has everything but the operators, as Sugar isn't in
      it either. (roadmap 2026-0053)
    - F13: CSV's DIAGNOSTICS, and the reference, give its messages for
      a field that's a reference, and for a wide character written
      raw, each word for word. (roadmap 2026-0053)
    - F15: xt/examples.t runs each of the reference's examples, and
      checks it prints what its comments say, as xt/howto.t does the
      how-to's, and compiles each of the README's. Four examples were
      made to run on their own; 11 that can't are marked, and checked
      by hand. All 81 that run print what they say. (roadmap 2026-0053)
    - F16: xt/agreement.t runs 3,000 random pipelines, from a fixed
      seed, pushed, pulled, read with all and fed, and fails if any way
      gives other values. (roadmap 2026-0053)
    - Every module has a $VERSION, the distribution's, so a caller can
      ask for the version a module's feature came in, as use
      Ekorn::Pipeline::Tooling v0.3.16 qw(:all). Only Ekorn::Pipeline
      had one. (roadmap 2026-0053)
    - F17: the 41 patterns in the tests of JSON, CSV and a source's
      original that lacked the x flag, against the house style, have
      it. (roadmap 2026-0053)

v0.3.15 2026-11-25

    Ekorn::Pipeline::Tooling exports result_number and result_original,
    in :context and :all, as DBIx::Squirrel::Tooling does the same
    functions from its v3.6.1.

    It ships roadmap 2026-0051. It only adds.

    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Tooling: a :context tag, holding result_number
      and result_original, from Ekorn::Pipeline::Context, and both in
      :all. They were left out from v0.3.11, as DBIx::Squirrel::Tooling
      exported different functions by those names, and a program
      importing both would have got one or the other. From its v3.6.1,
      both of its are Ekorn's own, so the two Toolings agree on every
      name. (roadmap 2026-0051)
    - The reference, the how-to, the README and Context's POD say so.

v0.3.14 2026-11-24

    A list sink, such as keep_all, is about a quarter quicker read by
    an iterator's all, and over a third quicker pushed, as DBIx-Squirrel
    found it the slowest way to read every row.

    It ships no roadmap item, and changes nothing a run gives: only how
    fast all collects it.

    - Where an iterator's all, or a pushed run, collects a run's
      values, the values after the first that the last part gives at
      once, a list sink's result, a last gather's flush, or a last
      stage returning several, go straight to what it returns, in
      order, rather than each wrapped and queued, then taken off the
      queue one at a time, as the run's next still does. Through one
      stage that does nothing, 300,000 values, on Perl 5.36 and 5.44:
      keep_all read by all, 1,105 to 845 ns a value, and 1,186 to 914;
      pushed, 1,000 to 625, and 1,090 to 660; a last stage returning
      three values, 1,625 to 1,180, and 1,762 to 1,285. Runs that don't
      cost the same.

v0.3.13 2026-11-24

    A source can give the original its values were made from, such as
    the row a statement fetched, and result_original gives it, through
    chains of iterators too. So DBIx::Squirrel's result_original can
    be Ekorn's own, and the two can share one name in their Toolings.

    It ships roadmap 2026-0052. It only adds: a source that doesn't
    give one runs as before.

    - A source's set-up, or EKORN_SOURCE, may return an original, by
      name or fourth in a list: a code reference returning the original
      of the value it gave last. result_original calls it, only when
      asked, in place of the value the run's source gave; a flush's
      values have none, whatever it says. An iterator read as a source
      passes its source's original on, as it does its context. One
      that isn't a code reference croaks "Expected the source's
      original to be a code reference". (roadmap 2026-0052)
    - The POD of Ekorn::Pipeline::Source, Factories, Iterator and
      Context, the reference and the how-to say so.

v0.3.12 2026-11-24

    A fix to v0.3.11's Ekorn::Pipeline::Context: its functions now
    parse as terms, as DBIx-Squirrel found they didn't.

    It ships no roadmap item, and only fixes.

    - Fixed: result_number and result_original had no prototype, as
      the () of a sub under use v5.36 is a signature, not one. So a
      bare call took what followed as its arguments: result_number + 1
      died with "Too many arguments", and result_number // 'none'
      began a pattern. Each now has an empty prototype too, as
      DBIx::Squirrel's functions of the same names have, so its
      Context can export Ekorn's own.

v0.3.11 2026-11-24

    Ekorn::Pipeline::Context tells a stage which result it's making,
    and from what: each run counts its results, and keeps the value
    each is made from, so a stage can number what it gives, or reach
    the value as it came in, whatever stages changed it since.
    DBIx::Squirrel's functions of the same names can be built on it,
    and drop the stage it adds to count, which costs it 200 to 260 ns
    a row.

    It ships roadmap 2026-0046. It only adds, but every run now counts,
    whether its stages ask or not.

    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Context: result_number, the run's results so
      far, plus one, a result being a value that leaves the last stage
      or reaches a sink; and result_original, the value the run's
      source gave, or that was pushed, before any stage changed it,
      undef for a flush's values. Each run counts its own: a pipeline
      run inside a stage, a tee's branch and an iterator read as a
      source each count from 1. Both are undef outside a run, and in
      an end. (roadmap 2026-0046)
    - Every run counts its results, which costs a value 15 to 75 ns
      through one stage that does nothing, on Perl 5.36 and 5.44: 1 to
      3% for an iterator's all, 3 to 12% for a pushed run, and 6 to 8%
      for an iterator's next. Less through stages that do work. Roadmap
      2026-0046's record has the measurements, and why every run
      counts.
    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Tooling doesn't export them, in v0.3.x:
      DBIx::Squirrel::Tooling exports functions of the same names, and
      DBIx-Squirrel v3.5.0's tests fail if both export different subs
      by one name. Import them from Ekorn::Pipeline::Context.
    - The reference has a section on them, and the how-to a recipe.

v0.3.10 2026-11-24

    Ekorn::Pipeline::CSV reads and writes CSV and TSV files, as JSON's
    module does JSON: a source or a sink given a file, and a stage
    given neither, with Text::CSV, which Ekorn recommends rather than
    requires. And a stop after a tee no longer loses what the tee's
    branch holds.

    It ships roadmap 2026-0049 and 2026-0050. It adds, and fixes one
    thing.

    - Ekorn::Pipeline::CSV: from_csv and to_csv. Rows are arrays, or
      hashes keyed by a header, whose names, munged if asked, as
      Text::CSV's munge_column_names does, are the run's columns, as
      DBIx::Squirrel's are; to_csv writes a header, and hashes in its
      order. Files are UTF-8 unless encoding says. A row that won't
      parse, text that won't decode, and a character a file's encoding
      can't hold each croak at their row, rather than ending the run
      quietly or writing something else. Text::CSV is loaded when
      either is first called, and its absence croaks then.
      (roadmap 2026-0049)
    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Tooling exports both in :csv, and in :all.
    - The reference and the how-to describe them, and the how-to's
      "Tell the stages about the run" points to from_csv.
    - Fixed: when a stop after a tee, such as take's or an iterator's
      first, ended the main run, the tee's branch was told it stopped,
      and its flushes weren't called, so what they held, such as a
      batch's last rows bound for a writer, was lost without a word.
      The tee now closes its branch in its end, so its flushes run and
      its ends are told done. A main run that dies still only finishes
      it. A tee whose branch has a flush now has an end too.
      (roadmap 2026-0050)

v0.3.9 2026-11-23

    v0.3.8's documentation, on CPAN, as v0.3.8 never reached it: it was
    tagged, but a word in the reference failed Minilla's POD spelling
    test as its tarball was built, so it wasn't uploaded. What v0.3.8's
    entry in Changes says is new is new to CPAN here.

    It ships no roadmap item, and changes only the documentation: the
    code is v0.3.7's.

    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Manual::Reference lists "setlist", a word its
      examples use, among its stopwords.

v0.3.8 2026-11-23

    The reference's index is for looking a name up, and the text before
    it for seeing it used. Each entry in the index has a synopsis of
    all the name's forms, what it does, whether it's exported by
    default or upon request, and a link to the section that shows it
    in use; those sections show it, with examples on one catalogue of
    tracks, rather than define it again.

    It ships no roadmap item, and changes only the documentation: the
    code is v0.3.7's.

    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Manual::Reference, and docs/REFERENCE.md: each
      index entry has a synopsis, and a link to the name in use. Names
      used together are shown together: Flow's stages in "Taking and
      dropping", Batch's in "Batching and collecting", peek and
      count_into in "Taps", Stop and throw in "What a stop does", pipe
      and tap in one section. The tables that only defined names, and
      the Exports and The modules sections, are gone, as the index
      holds what they did. The manuals have index entries.
    - The README shows Ekorn-Pipeline's logo, on GitHub; POD readers
      no longer show its badges' links as text.

v0.3.7 2026-11-18

    The reference ends in an index: each module, A to Z, with its
    functions and methods, A to Z, what each does, and how it's
    exported, linked to where it's described.

    It ships no roadmap item, and changes only the documentation: the
    code is v0.3.6's.

    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Manual::Reference, and docs/REFERENCE.md, have
      an Index, after The modules.

v0.3.6 2026-11-17

    A pipeline can be fed its values as they arrive, from a library
    that calls you back, an event loop, or a stage running its values
    through a pipeline of its own; and tee, built on that, is a tap of
    any shape, running each value through a branch of stages beside
    the main run.

    It ships roadmap 2026-0044 and 2026-0048. It only adds: pushed and
    pulled runs, the messages, and the order things run in are as they
    were, and no slower; a push through plain stages is a little faster.

    - $pipeline->feed returns an Ekorn::Pipeline::Feed, one run, made
      then: push runs values through the stages, and returns what came
      out of them; close ends the input, and returns what the flushes
      give, and a sink's result; finish ends the run as from outside;
      ended says how it ended. Fed the same values, a feed gives what
      a call gives, a push at a time. A stop ends the run in the push
      that meets it. Once the run has ended, a push or a close croaks,
      "Expected a feed still open, but its run has ended", so values
      can't go nowhere unnoticed; a push, close or finish from inside
      the feed's own run croaks too. A feed has no context of its own:
      its stages see the context of the code pushing into it, none from
      outside a run. A push costs about 1.3 µs a value through a bare
      stage, and 2 µs through a gather with a flush, on Perl 5.36 and
      5.44. The user guide shows a library's callbacks feeding a
      function's caller's stages, a tee running values through a
      branch of stages beside the main run, and a stage running each
      key's values through a run of their own. (roadmap 2026-0044)
    - tee(@stages), in Ekorn::Pipeline::Stages::Taps, exported on
      request and in :all and Ekorn::Pipeline::Tooling's :taps, runs
      each value through a branch of the stages, and passes it on
      unchanged; what comes out of the branch is dropped. The branch is
      one run of its own, beside the main run, fed by a feed made for
      each main run, so its stages keep their state across the values.
      A stop in it, such as take's, ends only the branch. A death in it
      kills the run, named as the tee's stage, then the branch's within
      it. Its flushes are called in the tee's flush, and its ends as its
      run ends, or, if the main run ended first, in the tee's end, told
      stopped. The branch sees the main run's context, so stages that
      read it, such as a statement's columns, work there. It takes
      whatever new takes, a pipeline or a sink last included. A tee has
      a flush and an end only if its branch needs them, so a branch of
      plain stages leaves the main run on the plain loop, and costs
      about 1.3 µs a value, against peek's 0.4. (roadmap 2026-0048)
    - A push through plain stages, and through stages made with gather
      that have no flush or end, copies its values once less, costing
      about 5% less a value: through one plain stage, 332 to 335 ns,
      not 348 to 360, on Perl 5.36, and 370 to 375, not 395 to 411, on
      5.44.

v0.3.5 2026-11-16

    Pushing values through take, drop and the other stages made with
    gather costs 40 to 45% less a value, and a short push less than
    half what it did.

    It ships roadmap 2026-0041. Nothing changes but speed: the values,
    the messages, the order things run in, and what each part is told
    are as they were.

    - A push, $pipeline->(@values), takes the plain loop whenever the
      stages made for its run have no flush or end, as take, drop,
      take_single, drop_while, count_into and most stages made with
      gather haven't; until now any stage made for a run sent it the
      longer way. A stage made with gather then costs a pushed value
      no more than a plain one: through take, about 400 ns a value,
      not 720, on Perl 5.36, and 440, not 775, on 5.44; and a push of
      one value through take(5) about 5 µs, not 14. Pulled runs, and
      runs with a flush, an end or a sink, such as batch, take_last
      and the sinks, are as they were. (roadmap 2026-0041)

v0.3.4 2026-11-16

    The canned stages, sources and sinks cost a little less a value.

    It ships no roadmap item: the change came of a message from
    DBIx-Squirrel. Nothing else changes: the values, the messages and
    the order things run in are as they were.

    - The closures that the canned stages, sources and sinks run for
      each value read it without a signature, which cost about 19 ns
      a call: take, drop, take_last, take_single, count_into, dump_to,
      batch, collect, the sinks of Ekorn::Pipeline::Sinks, from_array,
      from_fh, from_file, counter, and Ekorn::Pipeline::JSON's. Pushed
      through one of them, a value costs 15 to 30 ns less, 2 to 4%, on
      Perl 5.36 and 5.44. take_if, drop_if, take_while, drop_while and
      peek still copy the value before giving it to their block, so a
      block that changes its argument changes nothing passed on.

v0.3.3 2026-11-16

    A run costs about half what it did to start and end, so a short
    run, such as an iterator reading one row, costs 7 to 8 µs less.

    It ships change record 2026-0040, a task from DBIx-Squirrel, which
    profiled its handles. Nothing changes but speed: the values, the
    messages, the order things run in, and what each part is told are
    as they were.

    - An iterator whose run has no flush, no stage's end and no sink,
      as most have, takes a shorter way, with none of their
      bookkeeping: with no stages, $p->iterator($code) let go costs
      about 8 µs, not 15, and $p->iterator(source {...})->all about 12
      µs, not 20, on Perl 5.36 and 5.44 alike. Every run is a little
      cheaper to set up, and a plain run pulling many values is 3 to 6%
      faster a value. (change record 2026-0040)

v0.3.2 2026-11-15

    dump_to redacts the secrets in what it writes, whenever
    Text::Redact::Secrets is installed, so a dump sent to a log
    doesn't leak a password, a token or a DSN.

    It ships roadmap 2026-0038. Nothing changes unless
    Text::Redact::Secrets is installed: then dumps are redacted, and
    a module that's there but broken makes dump_to croak, with a new
    message. What a stage sees, and every message there was before,
    are as they were.

    - dump_to redacts with Text::Redact::Secrets' redact when it's
      installed, a v1.x, and passes a self-test, redacting a few
      made-up secrets in a dump: each secret it finds in what's
      written, such as a password, a token or the password in a DSN,
      is written as *REDACTED*. Only the dump is redacted: the value
      passed on is as it came. The module is looked for once, at the
      first dump. If it's there but fails to load, isn't a v1.x, or
      fails the test, dump_to croaks, on that dump and each after,
      rather than write a dump that may hold secrets, with a new
      message, "Text::Redact::Secrets ..., so dump_to won't write a
      dump that may hold secrets". local
      $Ekorn::Pipeline::Stages::Taps::DumpSecrets = 1 writes secrets as
      they are, read as each value is dumped; while it's true, the
      module is neither loaded nor tested. It isn't a dependency:
      cpanfile recommends it, so installers that take recommendations
      install it. The user guide and the reference show it. (roadmap
      2026-0038)

v0.3.1 2026-11-14

    Sources, stages, gathers and sinks, each made afresh for a run and
    told how it ended, with words to make them and | to join them.

    Pipelines can be made with pipeline(), and named, and a function
    whose values can be code references can take its stages as a
    pipeline given last. pipeline() is one of the words in
    Ekorn::Pipeline::Factories, short functions that make Ekorn's
    types: stage, gather, source, generator with yield, sink_one and
    sink_many. The README's CPAN badge keeps up with releases, and the
    how-to says where a stage belongs. Stages::Flow has take_first,
    take_last and take_single, and take_if and drop_if, which filter.
    Ekorn::Pipeline::Tooling exports every stage, source, sink and
    word from one module, by tags. Ekorn::Pipeline::JSON reads and
    writes JSON and JSON Lines, as sources, stages or sinks, and
    Ekorn::Pipeline::Sugar names pipelines as the program compiles,
    each with a function of its name.

    A run's parts can be made afresh for it, and told how it ended:
    sources that close what they open, however the run ends; sinks
    that consume every value and give a result, committing only when
    the run is done; and gathers with an end as well as a flush, what
    v0.2.0 called the end function; Sources and Sinks have them
    ready-made, and Operators joins them with |. A part's set-up, or
    a package's role method, can return its parts by name, as a hash
    reference, rather than as a list in a set order. An iterator is an
    object, which can read what's left in one call, end its run early,
    and say how it ended, and which ends its run when it's let go.

    Pushing values through plain subs is several times faster, reading
    an iterator's values in one call a tenth faster, and a stop about
    three times cheaper.

    It ships nineteen roadmap items, 2026-0009, 2026-0010, 2026-0011,
    2026-0013, 2026-0014, 2026-0016, 2026-0017, 2026-0018, 2026-0021,
    2026-0024, 2026-0025, 2026-0026, 2026-0027, 2026-0028, 2026-0029,
    2026-0030, 2026-0031, 2026-0032 and 2026-0034. It changes what
    some code sees, as the entries marked "Changed" say: a gather, an
    iterator, every canned stage, and the stages a pipeline gives back
    are objects, called as code references, not code references
    themselves; a gather's message names the flush and the end; a
    stage's error names the named pipeline it's in; the messages for
    what isn't a stage or a source say what one is; and a pipeline
    named alone, pipeline('clean'), is the pipeline itself, so its
    stages are numbered where it's given. Otherwise, existing code
    runs as before.

    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Sugar names pipelines as the program compiles:
      use Ekorn::Pipeline::Sugar pipeline => {name => [@stages]}, or an
      array of pairs, names each in the package that uses it, and gives
      that package a function of each name, which runs values through
      the pipeline, and, given a pipeline last, through that too. A
      definition can name any other in the same use, in either order.
      Mistakes, such as a name taken already, or a circle of names,
      croak as the use compiles. import_into does the same for a
      package given, for other modules' sugar. Sub::Util, which comes
      with Perl, is a new dependency. (roadmap 2026-0010)
    - Ekorn::Pipeline::JSON makes from_json and to_json, for one JSON
      document, and from_jsonl and to_jsonl, for JSON Lines: each a
      source or a sink given a file or a filehandle, and a stage given
      neither. from_json gives an array's elements, or the document
      itself; from_jsonl gives each line's document, read a block at a
      time, as values are pulled. to_json writes every value as one
      array, and to_jsonl a value a line. As stages, each passes on
      what a file would hold, so to_json and from_json undo each
      other, as to_jsonl and from_jsonl do. The encoder is
      JSON::MaybeXS's, configured by a json option, or replaced by an
      object of the caller's. Tooling exports all four, by a :json
      tag. JSON::MaybeXS is a new dependency. The user guide reads and
      writes JSON in chapters 5 and 6, and walks through how a module
      like it is put together in chapter 10. (roadmap 2026-0034)
    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Tooling exports what callers use to do work,
      from one module, so there's nothing to remember about which
      module makes which: tags :core (Stop and gather), :flow, :batch
      and :taps, :stages for the three, :sources, :sinks, :words (the
      factories) and :all. Each tag holds every name its module
      exports, as the same sub, and nothing is exported by default.
      It leaves out is_stage, is_sink and croakf, which are for
      extending the package, and the operators, which stay a pragma.
      The user guide's examples, the README's and the reference's
      At a glance import from it. (roadmap 2026-0031)
    - The reference has a section, Making each kind of part, for
      developers: a table of what each piece of a part, its set-up,
      step, flush, end and context, is called with, returns, and
      whether it may stop the run, and a worked example of each kind
      of part, made with a word and as a class, from a stage with
      state to an object that's both a source and a sink, committing
      only a run that went through. Every example in the README, the
      user guide, the reference and the modules' POD writes a
      pipeline of two or more stages a stage to a line, and
      paragraphs are filled evenly again.
    - The role methods, by which any object can be a source, a stage
      or a sink, are named in capitals, EKORN_SOURCE, EKORN_STAGE,
      EKORN_SINK_ONE and EKORN_SINK_MANY, as Perl names subs that its
      runtime calls, not their caller, such as DESTROY, and as CPAN
      names hooks for another package, such as TO_JSON. No release had
      them in lower case (roadmap 2026-0027).
    - A set-up, a gather's, a source's or a sink's, and a role method,
      EKORN_SOURCE, EKORN_STAGE, EKORN_SINK_ONE or EKORN_SINK_MANY,
      can return its parts by name, as a hash reference with the keys
      step, flush, end and context, giving only those it has, in place
      of a list in a set order with undef for one left out before one
      given: gather { {step => $step, end => $end} }. Other keys are
      ignored, and a hash without what its part needs croaks, as a
      list does. The list still works. Ekorn's own sources, sinks and
      stages, and an iterator's EKORN_SOURCE, return theirs by name
      (roadmap 2026-0026).
    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Sources has ready-made sources, needing only
      Perl, none exported by default: from_array(@values); from_fh($fh),
      a filehandle's records, as $/ divides them, chomped, left open;
      from_file($path, $layers), a file's records, opened through the
      I/O layers given, if any, for each run, and closed however it
      ends; and counter($start, $step), without end. With $/
      undefined, from_fh and from_file slurp, an empty file giving
      none. (roadmap 2026-0016)
    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Sinks has ready-made sinks, needing only Perl,
      none exported by default: keep_all, every item; keep($n), the
      first $n, stopping there; keep_first, stopping at it;
      keep_single, warning at a second; keep_last; count_items; and
      to_fh($fh) and to_file($path, $layers), writing each item
      followed by $/, as from_fh and from_file take it off, and giving
      how many. to_file opens its file for each run, through the I/O
      layers given, if any, and closes it however the run ends.
      (roadmap 2026-0017)
    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Operators, used in a scope, puts |, |= and @{}
      in force there for Ekorn's types: $source | $stage | $sink gives
      the sink's result, $stage | $stage a chain of stages, $source |
      $stage a source, and @{ $source } every value, read through. A
      chain starts with a source or a stage made with a word, never a
      plain code reference, whose | is Perl's bitwise OR. Out of
      scope, they croak "Expected use Ekorn::Pipeline::Operators in
      scope, for %s". A package's own source class chains by
      overloading | and @{} with Operators' chain and drain. (roadmap
      2026-0018)
    - Changed: a stage that isn't one croaks "Expected stage #%d to be
      a stage: a code reference, a pipeline, or an object offering a
      stage's role method", where it said "Expected stage #%d to be a
      code reference or a pipeline", and, given to pipeline or named,
      "... a pipeline or its name, or an object offering a stage's role
      method"; both left out the parts made for a run. A pipeline whose
      overridden stages method returns something that isn't a stage has
      a message of its own, "Expected stage #%d, a pipeline, to give
      only stages from its stages method", in place of one saying it
      should be a pipeline. Stage->new, given one of Ekorn's types,
      croaks "Expected a code reference, not one of Ekorn's types".
    - Changed: iterator, given something other than a source, croaks
      "Expected a source: a code reference, or an object offering
      EKORN_SOURCE", where it said "Expected a code reference", which
      was accurate only until sources could be objects.
    - Changed: a stage that dies within a named pipeline is named with
      it: after its place, the innermost named pipeline and the
      stage's place there, "Pipeline stage #1.2 (main::clean#2)
      failed at ...", and an end, "Pipeline end of stage #1.2
      (main::clean#2) failed at ...". A stage of no named pipeline is
      named as before. A pipeline's name method gives its first name,
      qualified, or undef. (roadmap 2026-0013)
    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Stages::Flow exports two more stages on request:
      take_if { ... }, passing on each value the block is true of, and
      drop_if { ... }, each it's false of, testing every value, as grep
      does, where take_while and drop_while stop testing at the first
      value that fails. A stage ending in a test passes on true or
      false; take_if passes on the value. (roadmap 2026-0030)
    - Faster: a pipeline whose stages are all plain subs, called with
      values, runs them in a plain loop, with none of the machinery
      for flushes, ends and sinks: a call of a few values is about
      twelve times faster, and a call of many through one stage three
      and a half times. Every other run is a little faster too: a push
      hands its values over at once, and less is done for each value.
      Nothing else changes. (roadmap 2026-0028)
    - Faster: pulled runs, as DBIx-Squirrel measured them. An
      iterator's all collects every value in one pass of the run,
      rather than a call for each, about a tenth faster through one
      plain stage, and each pull copies less. Nothing else changes.
      (roadmap 2026-0032)
    - Faster: a stop, thrown with die rather than Carp's croak, which
      added nothing to it but cost, about three times cheaper, as
      DBIx-Squirrel measured it. A catcher gets the same object as
      before.
    - Changed: pipeline_in, and so the word pipeline, given one name
      and nothing else, returns the pipeline named itself, not a new
      pipeline holding it, if it's of the invocant's class or a
      subclass. So a stage of a named pipeline given second is
      "Pipeline stage #2.2 (main::clean#2)", not "#2.1.2". named on a
      class still makes a new pipeline, even of one name. (roadmap
      2026-0029)
    - The message for a gather's block that returns the wrong things
      now ends "and optionally a flush and an end", where it ended
      "and optionally an end function": what v0.2.0 called the end
      function is now the flush, and an end, told how the run ended,
      may follow it. Code matching the old wording must change.
      (roadmap 2026-0014)
    - A run's parts can be made for it. Ekorn::Pipeline::Source makes
      a source from a set-up, called at the start of each run, which
      returns its step and, optionally, its end and a context;
      iterator takes one, or anything offering EKORN_SOURCE. A source
      given to new as a stage croaks. (roadmap 2026-0014)
    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Sink makes sinks, with new_sink_one, giving one
      value, and new_sink_many, giving a list: a set-up returns the
      step, the flush, giving the result, and optionally an end. A
      pipeline ending in a sink gives its result: in scalar context, a
      sink of one's value, or a sink of many's list as an array
      reference. is_sink, exported on request, says "one", "many" or
      false. (roadmap 2026-0014)
    - Each end, a source's, a gather's or a sink's, is called once,
      when the run has ended, however it ended, told "done", "stopped"
      or "died", the source's first. A run that dies calls every end,
      told "died", before throwing its error; an end that dies is
      reported as "Pipeline end of %s failed at %s line %d:", or, in a
      run already dying, warns "The end of %s died while the run was
      already dying: %s". A gather's block may return an end, third,
      after its flush. (roadmap 2026-0014)
    - new takes, as a stage, any object offering EKORN_STAGE,
      EKORN_SINK_ONE or EKORN_SINK_MANY, called at the start of each
      run, so a package can deliver parts for Ekorn without using its
      classes; one offering more than one croaks. is_stage is true for
      them. run_context gives a stage the context its run's source
      gave. (roadmap 2026-0014)
    - Changed: iterator returns an Ekorn::Pipeline::Iterator, an object
      called as a code reference, as before, with all, first, single,
      finish and ended. Let go before its values run out, it ends its
      run, so a source closes what it opened. An iterator can be a
      source for another pipeline, once. (roadmap 2026-0014)
    - Changed: a gather is an Ekorn::Pipeline::Gather object, called
      as a code reference, not a blessed code reference, and stages
      returns a pipeline's stages as objects: a code reference given
      to new comes back as an Ekorn::Pipeline::Stage of it. Code
      testing them with is_coderef or ref sees the change. (roadmap
      2026-0014)
    - New messages, for sources, sinks, parts and ends, are listed in
      the POD's DIAGNOSTICS and the reference's Diagnostics. (roadmap
      2026-0014)
    - The how-to, Ekorn::Pipeline::Manual::HowTo, is rewritten as a
      user guide. It tours the package from the bottom up, a layer at
      a time: stages, pipelines pushed and pulled, stopping, parts made
      for each run, sources, sinks, composition, the operators,
      functions that take stages, and parts for other packages; then
      errors and debugging, and the questions people ask first, such
      as how fast it is, how it compares with map, grep, options and
      callbacks, and where it falls short. Each of its examples runs,
      and prints what it says.
    - The README is rewritten for this release. It shows the shape of
      a run, from a source through stages to a sink, with sections on
      parts made for each run, sources, sinks, joining them with |,
      named pipelines, and parts for other packages, and lists the
      modules that come with it.
    - The manuals' tables show a | written in them as |, not \|.
    - The reference has a section, Runs and how they end, and another,
      Delivering parts for Ekorn; the how-to has recipes for sources
      and sinks that close and commit however a run ends, and for
      reading an iterator's values in one call. (roadmap 2026-0014)
    - pipeline(), exported on request by Ekorn::Pipeline::Factories,
      makes a new pipeline from stages, as new does, but a stage can
      also be the name of a pipeline: pipeline('clean', take(5)). It's
      Ekorn::Pipeline->pipeline_in(scalar caller, @stages), the one
      method that reads names, making its invocant's class. (roadmap
      2026-0011, 2026-0021)
    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Factories has the words that make Ekorn's
      types, each one line over a class method, none exported by
      default: pipeline, stage, gather (Gather's own), source,
      generator and yield, sink_one and sink_many. generator gives a
      source of what its block passes to yield, as a Python generator
      does, collecting it all in one step; yield outside one croaks
      "Expected yield to be called inside a generator". (roadmap
      2026-0021)
    - Ekorn::Pipeline->named(clean => @stages) defines a named
      pipeline, and $p->named('clean') names one you have. A name is
      kept in the caller's package unless it's qualified, whatever
      class defined it, is resolved when a pipeline is made, and can't
      be defined twice, or removed. Only pipeline() and named() read names: new
      doesn't, and is_stage stays false for a string. (roadmap
      2026-0011)
    - Five new messages, for pipeline() and named(): "Expected stage
      #%d to be a code reference, a pipeline or the name of one", "No
      pipeline named %s", "A pipeline named %s is already defined",
      "Expected a name, made of words joined by ::" and "Expected only
      a name, naming a pipeline you have". (roadmap 2026-0011)
    - The reference, the how-to and the README show a pipeline given
      last as the way to take stages after values that can be code
      references, and how to name a pipeline you use often. (roadmap
      2026-0011)
    - The reference has a section on the words, and its examples, and
      the how-to's, make sources and sinks with them. (roadmap
      2026-0021)

    - The README's CPAN badge comes from shields.io, which keeps up
      with a release within minutes, in place of badge.fury.io's, which
      GitHub showed out of date for hours. (roadmap 2026-0009)
    - The how-to has a recipe, "Decide where a stage belongs": under
      Ekorn::Pipeline::Stages, or with the module whose values it
      needs to know about. Its cpanfile example asks for v0.2.0.
    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Stages::Flow exports three more stages on
      request: take_first, which is take(1); take_last, passing on the
      last value once there are no more; and take_single, passing on
      the one value, and, at a second, warning "Expected only one item,
      but there were more" and stopping. Each, with take($n), is the
      stage of a keep sink, passing on what the sink would give.
      (roadmap 2026-0024)
    - Changed: every canned stage, in Stages::Flow, Batch and Taps, is
      an object called as a code reference: a Gather, or, for
      take_while, peek and dump_to, an Ekorn::Pipeline::Stage. Code
      testing one with ref or is_coderef sees the change. (roadmap
      2026-0024)

v0.2.0 2026-11-09

    Messages say a stage can be a code reference or a pipeline.

    It ships one roadmap item, 2026-0008. Two messages change, so it's
    v0.2.0: code that matches the old wording, as a string or an
    anchored pattern, needs updating. Nothing else changes in
    behaviour. DBIx::Squirrel signed this release off before it went
    out.

    - "Expected stage #%d to be a code reference" is now "Expected
      stage #%d to be a code reference or a pipeline". (roadmap
      2026-0008)
    - "Expected stage #%s, made with gather, to return one or two code
      references" is now "Expected stage #%s, made with gather, to
      return a code reference or a pipeline, and optionally an end
      function". (roadmap 2026-0008)
    - The DIAGNOSTICS, the reference, the how-to and the README say the
      same, and no longer explain the old wording. (roadmap 2026-0008)

v0.1.3 2026-11-09

    A pipeline can be used as a stage, and the docs are plainer.

    Pipelines can be built from other pipelines without ->stages, and a
    gather block can return one. The documentation is rewritten in
    plainer English.

    It ships three roadmap items, 2026-0004, 2026-0006 and 2026-0007.
    Existing code runs as before, and every message a pipeline gives
    today reads as before. The one exception is a subclass of
    Ekorn::Pipeline used with an import list, which now croaks, as
    below. DBIx::Squirrel signed this release off before it went out.

    - Ekorn::Pipeline->new accepts a pipeline as a stage, and splices
      its stages in where it stands. They run in the new pipeline's
      run, so a take inside limits the whole run. The stages come from
      the pipeline's stages method, so a subclass can override it.
      (roadmap 2026-0004)
    - In error messages, a stage inside a pipeline used as a stage is
      named by its position in each pipeline: "Pipeline stage #2.3
      failed", or "#1.2.3" three levels down. DIAGNOSTICS shows "#%s"
      for these messages. Other stages are named as before.
      (roadmap 2026-0004)
    - is_stage, exported by Ekorn::Pipeline on request, is true for
      anything new accepts as a stage: a code reference or a pipeline.
      Use it in a function that takes its stages from the end of its
      arguments, so it accepts pipelines too. Ask for v0.1.3 on the use
      line, as v0.1.2 lets "use Ekorn::Pipeline qw(is_stage)" compile.
      (roadmap 2026-0004)
    - Ekorn::Pipeline now has an import method, from Exporter, for
      is_stage. A subclass inherits it, so "use My::Subclass qw(name)"
      croaks unless My::Subclass exports that name; before, the list
      was ignored. (roadmap 2026-0004)
    - A gather block can return a pipeline as its stage. Its stages are
      made for each run in its place, gather stages inside it afresh,
      and the block's end function, if any, is called after theirs. A
      stage inside it is named as "#2.3" in messages. The message for a
      bad gather block is unchanged. (roadmap 2026-0007)
    - The README, the POD, the reference and the how-to are rewritten
      in shorter sentences, saying the same. Two out-of-date claims in
      the README are fixed: Ekorn::Pipeline is on CPAN, and
      DBIx::Squirrel uses it. (roadmap 2026-0006)
    - The README has MetaCPAN and Kwalitee badges.

v0.1.2 2026-11-08

    The reference and the how-to on MetaCPAN, as POD manuals.

    It adds documentation only: the code is as in v0.1.1, and it ships
    no roadmap items.

    - The reference and the how-to ship as POD,
      Ekorn::Pipeline::Manual::Reference and
      Ekorn::Pipeline::Manual::HowTo, made from their Markdown, so
      MetaCPAN and perldoc have them with the modules.

v0.1.1 2026-11-08

    The pipeline from DBIx-Squirrel v2.0.2, on its own, so that any
    function can take stages from its caller.

    It ships three roadmap items, settled after v0.1.0's trial: a
    pipeline's stages can be spliced into another, with the stages
    method (2026-0001); an iterator ends once a stage dies (2026-0002);
    and a pipeline's count in scalar context is promised (2026-0003).

    - Requires Perl 5.36 or later.
    - Ekorn::Pipeline runs values through a list of stages, each a code
      reference given one value, in $_ and as its argument, and
      returning nothing (a filter), one value (a map) or several (a
      flatMap). A pipeline is called with values, or pulls them from a
      source with an iterator, calling it only as results are wanted.
    - Once a stage dies, an iterator has ended, as a pushed run has:
      every call after returns an empty list, and neither the source
      nor any end function is called again. In v0.1.0's trial it
      carried on, without the value that died. (roadmap 2026-0002)
    - Called in scalar context, a pipeline returns how many results
      came out, as an array would; the POD now promises it.
      (roadmap 2026-0003)
    - A pipeline's stages method returns its stages, so they can be
      spliced into another pipeline, or given to any function that
      takes stages, running as if listed there. (roadmap 2026-0001)
    - SECURITY.md says how to report a vulnerability, privately, and
      CONTRIBUTING.md how to report a bug or ask for a feature; the
      distribution takes issues, not pull requests.
    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Stop ends a pipeline early, passing on any
      values it carries, after which the source isn't called again. A
      stop not made by throw, and so not an array, carries nothing.
    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Gather makes a stage afresh for each run, with
      an optional end function, called once the values run out.
    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Pipes gives a class pipe and tap methods, which
      run the object through stages.
    - Canned stages: take, drop, take_while and drop_while in
      Ekorn::Pipeline::Stages::Flow; batch and collect in
      Ekorn::Pipeline::Stages::Batch; peek, count_into and dump_to in
      Ekorn::Pipeline::Stages::Taps.
    - A stage that dies is named by its place, at the caller's line, and
      so are mistakes in making a pipeline or a canned stage.
    - Modules of stages can live under any namespace: general stages
      under Ekorn::Pipeline::Stages, and those for one kind of value or
      source with the module that knows about it, built from Gather,
      Stop and croakf, which reports at the caller's line.
    - Versions: a v0.1.x only fixes or adds. A change to how stages
      behave, or a public name dropped or renamed, waits for v0.2.0.
    - A how-to, docs/HOWTO.md, with recipes for callers, function
      authors and stage authors, and a reference, docs/REFERENCE.md,
      describing every module and how a pipeline behaves, with notes
      for stage authors. Both are on GitHub, linked from the README.
